Subject: [PATCH 09846/11362] [outer-loop] File the live-LLM corpus size: 2.6%
 of rows, and the filter that hides it

Measured across every rows*.json in the job scratch tree, deduplicated by
(run, game, seed, arm): 2212 rows, 63 with tokens_predicted > 0, 57 both
token-producing and llm_on_row_valid True.

The trap is the filter, not the number. llm_on_row_valid reads True on 1188
rows while only 63 produced a token, because it means "valid measurement of
its own arm" -- an LLM-off row is validly LLM-off. Anyone selecting live data
with it gets 1188 rows and believes they have plenty. Overstates live coverage
by roughly 19x. Filter on llm.tokens_predicted > 0.

Same class as levels_completed and preconditions_checked: a field whose name
suggests one thing and whose semantics are another. I reported "10/10 VALID"
from it repeatedly today. That was true of my own sweep, where every cell was
genuinely LLM-on, and would have been badly wrong as a corpus-wide filter.

Consequence worth stating: every characterization of live-agent behaviour rests
on tens of rows. Two game-specific patterns were claimed today from this base
and both were wrong. The 109 real induction-rejection events -- 1578 of 1687
skip events are disabled_by_env, meaning induction never ran -- spread across
seven reasons and 25 games at a median of 1-2 events per reason-game pair.

Distribution recorded so it is not re-derived. Also explains why the seed
question looked answerable and was not: five arm pairs is a substantial
fraction of the live corpus, not a small slice of it.

Memory: project_live_llm_corpus_is_tiny.

